  "slug": "microbiome-rhizosphere-34b7",
  "summary": "Generative and reinforcement learning optimizers are now being validated against docking, affinity estimation, and molecular dynamics checks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the coupling of learned generative or optimization models with physics-based scoring, sampling, or simulation for molecular design and pose prediction. UnibindMD chains BindCraft peptide design with AlphaFold3 and Boltz-2 re-prediction, PRODIGY affinity estimation, and 100 ns MD to validate Andes virus peptides, while the IL-23 p19 miniprotein work uses BindCraft hallucination with high ipSAE interfaces. AlphaVariant and AbOptimizer apply reinforcement learning with evolutionary or protein language model priors to enzyme and antibody optimization, and FragDockRL pairs a DQN search with tethered docking. On the physics side, a QUBO formulation solved by a quantum-classical hybrid solver is benchmarked against MIQP-based qFit-ligand, and multiscale kinetic Monte Carlo decomposes association into encounter, pocket entry, and binding stages. The newer contribution lies in methodological comparisons and multi-stage validation pipelines rather than in any single model.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-27"
}
